==Venue==
The final took place at the [[Khalifa International Stadium]] in [[Al Rayyan (city)|Al Rayyan]], Qatar. The venue previously hosted matches at the [[2011 AFC Asian Cup]], including the [[2011 AFC Asian Cup Final|final]], and was chosen as a venue for the [[2022 FIFA World Cup]]. Originally, the final (along with the second semi-final and third place match) was to be played at the [[Education City Stadium]], also located in Al Rayyan.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.fifa.com/clubworldcup/news/education-city-stadium-to-host-fifa-club-world-cup-qatar-2019tm-final |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190930082950/https://www.fifa.com/clubworldcup/news/education-city-stadium-to-host-fifa-club-world-cup-qatar-2019tm-final |url-status=dead |archive-date=30 September 2019 |title=Education City Stadium to host FIFA Club World Cup Qatar 2019 final |website=FIFA.com |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |date=30 September 2019 |access-date=30 September 2019}}</ref> However, the matches were moved after the opening of the Education City Stadium was postponed to early 2020.<ref>{{cite web |title=New FIFA Club World Cup champions to be crowned at Khalifa International Stadium |url=https://www.fifa.com/clubworldcup/news/new-fifa-club-world-cuptm-champions-to-be-crowned-at-khalifa-international-stadi |website=FIFA.com |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |date=7 December 2019 |access-date=7 December 2019}}</ref>

==Background==
The final was a rematch of the [[1981 Intercontinental Cup]], recognised by FIFA as a [[List of world champion football clubs|club world championship]]. Flamengo won the match 3–0 for their only club world title.<ref>{{cite news |last=Law |first=Joshua |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/blog/2019/dec/15/flamengo-liverpool-club-world-cup |title=Flamengo 3–0 Liverpool: the day Zico 'ran rings around the English' |publisher=[[The Guardian]] |date=15 December 2019 |access-date=18 December 2019}}</ref> Flamengo hadn't reached the final since while Liverpool had participated in two world championship matches in [[1984 Intercontinental Cup|1984]] and [[2005 FIFA Club World Championship Final|2005]], also losing both to [[Club Atlético Independiente|Independiente]] and [[São Paulo FC|São Paulo]] respectively.<ref>{{cite news |last=Fifield |first=Dominic |location=[[Yokohama]] |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2005/dec/19/newsstory.sport1 |title=Benítez makes beeline for Blatter after night of frustration |publisher=[[The Guardian]] |date=18 December 2005 |access-date=21 December 2019}}</ref>

