===Liverpool===
Liverpool qualified for the Club World Cup as champions of the [[2018–19 UEFA Champions League|UEFA Champions League]], having defeated [[Tottenham Hotspur F.C.|Tottenham Hotspur]] in the [[2019 UEFA Champions League Final|final]]. The club had previously played in the [[2005 FIFA Club World Championship|2005 Club World Championship]], finishing as runners-up to São Paulo.<ref>{{cite news |date=18 December 2019 |title=Club World Cup: Liverpool aim to become seventh European winners |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/025a-0e9f8fe852b8-ff94b0553a0e-1000--club-world-cup-firmino-wins-final-for-liverpool/ |work=[[UEFA]] |access-date=18 December 2019}}</ref> Due to their participation in the tournament, Liverpool were forced to field a squad of youth players for an [[2019–20 EFL Cup|EFL Cup]] tie against [[Aston Villa F.C.|Aston Villa]] due to the proximity of the two games, with the senior squad heading to Qatar to prepare while the reserves played in the cup game managed by U-23's manager [[Neil Critchley]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://talksport.com/football/634773/liverpool-aston-villa-carabao-cup-under-23s-jurgen-klopp/ |title=Liverpool to field ‘youthful’ team against Aston Villa in Carabao Cup quarter-final with Under-23s coach to replace Jurgen Klopp |first=Joe |last=Moore |publisher=TalkSPORT |date=26 November 2019 |access-date=25 August 2021}}</ref> As a result, they were beaten 5–0, the club's heaviest ever defeat in the competition <ref>{{cite news |last=Percy |first=John |date=17 December 2019 |title=Aston Villa hammer five past youngest-ever Liverpool side to progress to Carabao Cup semi-finals |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/football/2019/12/17/aston-villa-vs-liverpool-carabao-cup-quarter-final-live-score/ |work=The Telegraph |access-date=18 December 2019}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/50723225 |title=Aston Villa 5–0 Liverpool: Dean Smith's side overwhelm young Liverpool side |first=Simon |last=Stone |publisher=BBC Sport |date=17 December 2019}}</ref>

Liverpool entered with a [[bye (sports)|bye]] to the semi-finals, where they faced [[CONCACAF|North American]] champions [[C.F. Monterrey|Monterrey]] of Mexico. The Reds went ahead in the 12th minute with a strike by [[Naby Keïta]], who collected a pass from [[Mohamed Salah]] at the right side of the [[Penalty area|box]]. Monterrey equalised two minutes later as [[Rogelio Funes Mori]], who collected a rebound off [[Alisson]]'s save of a volley taken by [[Jesús Gallardo]]. Liverpool had several chances to retake the lead and switched to a new formation after half-time, but were unable to score and left themselves open to counter-attacks. Substitute [[Roberto Firmino]] scored the winning goal for Liverpool in the first minute of stoppage time, tapping in a pass from [[Trent Alexander-Arnold]] within the six-yard box.<ref>{{cite news |last=Hunter |first=Andy |date=18 December 2019 |title=Firmino's injury-time winner sends Liverpool into Club World Cup final |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2019/dec/18/monterrey-liverpool-fifa-club-world-cup-semi-final-match-report |work=The Guardian |access-date=18 December 2019}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Sanders |first=Emma |date=18 December 2019 |title=Monterrey 1–2 Liverpool |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/50817868 |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |access-date=18 December 2019}}</ref>

===Flamengo===
Flamengo qualified for the Club World Cup as winners of the [[2019 Copa Libertadores|Copa Libertadores]], defeating [[Club Atlético River Plate|River Plate]] with two last-minute goals by [[Gabriel Barbosa]] in the [[2019 Copa Libertadores Final|final]], which was played a month before the Club World Cup.<ref>{{cite news |date=23 November 2019 |title=Gabigol brace clinches Libertadores for Flamengo |url=https://www.fifa.com/clubworldcup/news/libertadores-final-report |work=FIFA.com |access-date=18 December 2019}}</ref>

Flamengo entered with a bye to their semi-final match against [[AFC Champions League]] winners [[Al Hilal SFC|Al Hilal]] of [[Saudi Arabia]]. Flamengo started poorly and nearly conceded to [[Bafétimbi Gomis]] in the 16th minute after a big rebound from goalkeeper [[Diego Alves]]. Two minutes later, [[Mohammed Al-Breik]] crossed low to [[Salem Al-Dawsari]] in the box who scored the opening goal. Flamengo did little to create good chances in the first half but returned with a different posture and higher defensive line in the second half. Within three minutes, Flamengo's attacking trio leveled the score: Gabriel Barbosa found [[Bruno Henrique Pinto|Bruno Henrique]] open inside the box, who rolled the ball across to [[Giorgian De Arrascaeta]] for the equalizer. [[Diego (footballer, born 1985)|Diego]] came on in substitute for [[Gerson (footballer, born 1997)|Gerson]] and initiated the play in the 78th minute that resulted in [[Rafinha (footballer, born 1985)|Rafinha]]'s cross to Bruno Henrique for the go-ahead header. Three minutes later, Diego found Bruno Henrique in the box whose cross was deflected in by [[Ali Al-Bulaihi]] for an own-goal. Moments later, [[André Carrillo]] of Al-Hilal was sent off after hitting De Arrascaeta. Flamengo advanced with the 3–1 victory.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://globoesporte.globo.com/futebol/mundial-de-clubes/jogo/17-12-2019/flamengo-alhilal.ghtml |title=Flamengo 3 x 1 Al Hilal – Mundial de Clubes 2019 Semifinal – Tempo Real – Globo Esporte |website=globoesporte.com |language=pt-br|access-date=2019-12-18}}</ref>

==Match==

=== Summary ===
[[Roberto Firmino]] nearly scored for Liverpool in the first minute of the match, as the ball was lifted to him past the defense but his shot sailed high. Shortly after, [[Jordan Henderson]] and [[Trent Alexander-Arnold]] of Liverpool took shots of their own. Both teams continued equally matched, with Flamengo having a spell of possession after a rocky start and maintaining nearly sixty percent possession in the half.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://globoesporte.globo.com/futebol/mundial-de-clubes/jogo/21-12-2019/Liverpool-flamengo.ghtml |title=Liverpool 1 x 0 Flamengo – Mundial de Clubes 2019 Final – Tempo Real |website=Globo Esporte |language=pt-br|access-date=24 December 2019}}</ref> At the other end, [[Bruno Henrique (footballer, born 1990)|Bruno Henrique]] had several sights of goal, though none quite as dangerous as Liverpool's.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/live/2019/dec/21/liverpool-v-flamengo-club-world-cup-final-2019-live?page=with%3Ablock-5dfe5cd28f08c3972263ec4c |title=Liverpool 1–0 Flamengo: Club World Cup final 2019 – as it happened! (4 of 4) |last=Burnton |first=Simon |date=21 December 2019 |work=The Guardian|access-date=24 December 2019 |issn=0261-3077}}</ref>

Two minutes into the second half, Firmino nearly scored again, this time hitting the inside of the left post.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/live/2019/dec/21/liverpool-v-flamengo-club-world-cup-final-2019-live?page=with%3Ablock-5dfe6c368f087e8308e6283f&filterKeyEvents=false#liveblog-navigation |title=Liverpool 1–0 Flamengo: Club World Cup final 2019 – as it happened! (3 of 4) |last=Burnton |first=Simon |date=21 December 2019 |work=The Guardian|access-date=24 December 2019 |issn=0261-3077}}</ref> In the 53rd minute, Flamengo's [[Gabriel Barbosa]] shot high, and later forced a diving save from goalkeeper [[Alisson Becker|Alisson]] with a shot from inside the 18-yard box. In the 73rd minute, [[Alex Oxlade-Chamberlain]] was injured and came off for [[Adam Lallana]]. Flamengo brought on [[Vitinho (footballer, born October 1993)|Vitinho]] and [[Diego (footballer, born 1985)|Diego]] in place of [[Giorgian de Arrascaeta]] and [[Éverton Ribeiro]]. In the 83rd minute, Liverpool had their first dangerous shot on goal: a shot from Henderson fed by [[Mohamed Salah]] that was tipped over the frame by goalkeeper [[Diego Alves]]. Moments into stoppage time, Liverpool was awarded a penalty after [[Rafinha (footballer, born 1985)|Rafinha]] clipped the trailing foot of [[Sadio Mané]] free on goal on the edge of the penalty area. The decision was taken to [[Video assistant referee|video review]] where it appeared that the foul occurred outside the box and would result in a free kick, however the foul was waved off completely and play resumed with Flamengo.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://globoesporte.globo.com/futebol/mundial-de-clubes/jogo/21-12-2019/Liverpool-flamengo.ghtml |title=Liverpool 1 x 0 Flamengo – Mundial de Clubes 2019 Final – Tempo Real |website=Globo Esporte |language=pt-br |date=21 December 2019 |access-date=24 December 2019}}</ref> Regulation time ended with the match still scoreless.

In the 99th minute, Liverpool scored the breakthrough goal. Henderson played a long ball forward to Mané which [[Rodrigo Caio]] could not deflect. One-on-one with Rafinha, Mané played to an approaching Firmino on his left who hesitated and scored around Diego Alves. For Liverpool, [[James Milner]] came on for [[Naby Keïta]], and for Flamengo [[Lincoln (footballer, born 2000)|Lincoln]] came on for midfielder [[Gerson (footballer, born 1997)|Gerson]].<ref name="Summary1">{{cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/live/2019/dec/21/liverpool-v-flamengo-club-world-cup-final-2019-live |title=Liverpool 1–0 Flamengo: Club World Cup final 2019 – as it happened! (1 of 4) |last=Burnton |first=Simon |date=21 December 2019 |work=The Guardian |access-date=24 December 2019 |issn=0261-3077}}</ref> In the second period of extra time, Flamengo found one dangerous opportunity to equalize in the 119th minute when a Vitinho cross rebounded back to him off a defender and he picked out Lincoln whose shot sailed high from ten yards out.<ref name="Summary1" />  Minutes later, the match ended and Liverpool were victors by a score of 1–0.<ref name="Firmino"/>

